# axcall hosts file.
#
# Names for stations, so software that only understands hostnames can reach a
# callsign. This is /etc/hosts for the packet network, and it exists for the
# same reason: the thing on the other side has an address that people do not
# want to type, and the software in between has no idea what it means.
#
# Copy to /etc/axcall/hosts for everyone, or ~/.config/axcall/hosts for
# yourself; the per-user file replaces same-named entries in the system one.
#
# Used by axsocks(1) to turn the name a SOCKS client asks for into a station to
# call.
#
# The first two columns are mandatory. The third names a port from the ports
# file, and is there to say which radio a station is on; "-" or nothing means
# whichever port the proxy was started on.
#
# A name that is not listed here but is itself a valid callsign is used as one,
# so "gb7rdg-1" needs no entry. List a name when you want a short or memorable
# form, or when the name and the callsign genuinely differ.

# name       callsign     port
#gb7rdg      GB7RDG       radio
#gb7cip      GB7CIP-1     radio
#bbs         M0LTE-1      radio
#lab         M0LTE-2      -
